      "title": "Vercel Chat SDK streams cross-platform generative UI via json-render",
      "summary": "Vercel updated Chat SDK with json-render, letting agents stream structured UI specs into chat environments. The model writes typed JSON converted into platform-native formats, rendering cards in Slack Block Kit, Microsoft Teams Adaptive Cards, or web components without custom bridges.",
      "key_facts": [
        "Streams typed JSON component specs converted into native multi-platform cards.",
        "Supported target formats include Slack Block Kit and Teams Adaptive Cards.",
        "Component catalog includes interactive tables, charts, and buttons."
      ],
      "design_implication": "Multi-platform chat experiences often force design teams to rebuild identical interactive components separately for Slack, Teams, and web windows. Streaming structured JSON component trees decouples agent output from platform-specific styling, allowing design tokens and layouts to render natively across target hosts without custom bridge code. Map common data display patterns like metric tables and status badges to standard JSON schemas that cross-platform adapters can reliably digest.",
      "implication_lens": "workflow",
      "visual_need": "illustrate",
      "source_name": "Vercel Blog",
      "source_url": "https://vercel.com/changelog/json-render-and-chat-sdk-agents",
      "source_date": "2026-11-06T21:00:00.000Z",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "Chat SDK agents can stream generative UI using json-render.",
          "quote": "You can now stream generative UI from your agent with .Chat SDKjson-render"
        },
        {
          "claim": "JSON specs convert into native formats like Slack Block Kit and Teams Adaptive Cards.",
          "quote": "With a typed catalog of components, the agent writes a JSON spec that converts into a Chat SDK card. Each adapter then posts the card in its platform’s native format, such as Block Kit on Slack and Adaptive Cards on Teams."
        }
      ],
