      "title": "Vercel CLI adds automatic expiration for ephemeral prototype deployments",
      "summary": "Vercel added an automatic expiration flag to its CLI deploy command. Expired deployments are deleted automatically, allowing design and engineering teams to distribute ephemeral interactive prototypes, client demos, and concept builds without accumulating manual cleanup overhead.",
      "key_facts": [
        "Adds --delete-project-after flag to the deploy command in Vercel CLI.",
        "Automatically deletes projects after durations like one day or one week.",
        "Flag applies when Vercel CLI creates the new project deployment."
      ],
      "design_implication": "A designer sharing live coded prototypes with stakeholders often leaves behind orphaned deployment URLs, stale staging links, and lingering cloud storage costs. Enforcing automatic expiration at deployment turns staging links into self-cleaning artifacts, making throwaway explorations safe to distribute widely without manual cleanup chores. Add predefined lifespan flags to internal prototyping runbooks so exploratory builds automatically self-destruct after review cycles conclude.",
      "implication_lens": "workflow",
      "visual_need": "none",
      "source_name": "Vercel Blog",
      "source_url": "https://vercel.com/changelog/vercel-cli-can-now-set-new-projects-to-expire",
      "source_date": "2026-11-06T18:00:00.000Z",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "Vercel CLI lets users set new projects to expire and delete automatically.",
          "quote": "You can now set a new project to expire when you deploy it with . Expired projects are deleted automatically, so prototypes, demos, and test apps don't need cleaning up by hand.Vercel CLI"
        },
        {
          "claim": "The feature is triggered via a command flag with duration limits.",
          "quote": "Run with and a duration:vercel deploy--delete-project-after"
        }
      ],
